  "model": "gpt-6-luna",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_luna_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B017",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "For the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B001",
    "B016"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I retain B017 as my preferred configuration. Optical lattice clocks turned control of atomic motion and frequency shifts into a mature precision measurement method. Independent development, repeated comparisons, and continuing refinement support the maturity of the clocks themselves, while their precision enables tests of fundamental physics. Katori's lattice-clock concept and Ye's complementary advances in realization provide a focused two-person attribution.",
    "case_against": "The historical boundary between the foundational lattice-clock advances and the wider development of optical frequency standards is not clean. The citation should recognize the clock concept and its realization without assigning these two people all later gains in precision or the supporting infrastructure.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson favors B001 for its specific theory-to-experiment arc, while noting that the boundary between the original quantum spin Hall work and the broader topological-insulator field is difficult to draw.",
        "response": "The focused prediction and realization make B001 a strong alternative. I still favor B017 because the independently developed clock approaches and sustained record of comparisons show that the focal method has matured in practice. The clock citation also has a clear center if it is kept to optical lattice clocks, rather than the full history of frequency standards."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll emphasizes Katori's lattice-trapping scheme and Ye's advances in clock realization and precision, and argues that a narrow citation can separate these contributions from the wider standards field.",
        "response": "I agree with that division of credit and with the need to keep the scope narrow. The independent work and repeated experimental refinement strengthen the case that this is a robust achievement, not simply a claim based on later record-setting performance. The historical record should still be checked to establish the relative weight of the original concept and its realization."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson recognizes the clocks' precision and maturity but questions whether the prize should cover their original invention or broader advances in optical frequency standards.",
        "response": "That distinction is central. I would limit the citation to the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks. Their maturity rests on independent development and continued comparison and refinement of the clock method; broader standards infrastructure need not be included in the award's scope."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The historical record should clarify the relative contributions of Katori's original lattice-clock concept and Ye's experimental realization and advances.",
      "The citation must distinguish the lattice-clock achievement from later improvements and enabling work across optical frequency standards."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "My profile notes long-standing collaboration with Anne L'Huillier and coauthored work with Ursula Keller's group. Neither is named in my preferred configuration. Keller is among the credited names for shortlisted B002, which I do not include in this configuration or its alternatives; this professional connection should be noted if B002 is considered."
  }
}
